                <pb facs="0278" n="182" sameAs="frus:frus1872p1/pg182"/>

                <head>No. 129.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Washburne to Mr. Fish.</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Extract.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1872-08-02"><hi rendition="#italic">August</hi> 2, 1872</date>.
                        (Received August 15.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 671.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: The National Assembly has substantially
                    completed its labors for the session, and has determined to adjourn over from
                    the 4th instant until the 11th of November. Mr. Thiers has greatly strengthened
                    himself in the country within the last few months, and it may be said that he is
                    now completely master of the situation. He goes to Trouville, on the sea-shore,
                    for a little rest, and after that it is said that he will go to Fontainebleau,
                    to remain until the assembly shall reconvene.</p>
                <p>The success of the three-milliard loan is something astonishing. It has been
                    covered twelve times and a half, and the French people are very much
                    rejoiced.</p>
                <p>I have. &amp;c,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>E. B. WASHBURNE.</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
